 Let the set of all students be $A$.
Let the set of MATHCOUNTS participants be $B$, and the set of science club participants be $C$.
We know:
- $|A| = 50$
- $|B| = 28$
- $|C| = 21$
- $|A \cap B \cap C| = 6$

By the inclusion-exclusion principle:
\begin{align*}
|A \cup B| &= |A| + |B| - |A \cap B| \\
\end{align*}
Substituting the given values:
\begin{align*}
|A \cup B| &= 50 + 28 - 6 \\
\end{align*}
Simplifying:
\begin{align*}
|A \cup B| &= 72
\end{align*}
The number of students who participate in either MATHCOUNTS or science club is 72.

Since 6 students participate in neither, the number of students who participate in both MATHCOUNTS and science club is:
\begin{align*}
|A \cap B| &= |A \cup B| - |A \cap C| \\
\end{align*}
Substituting the known values:
\begin{align*}
|A \cap B| &= 72 - 6 \\
\end{align*}
Simplifying:
\begin{align*}
|A \cap B| &= 66
\end{align*}

\boxed{66}

