First, we can use the quadratic formula to find the roots of the polynomial. The polynomial can be written in standard form as $z^4 + 4iz^3 - 6z^2 + (4i-1)z - i = 0$.

The roots of the polynomial can be found using the formula for quadratic equations:

\begin{align*}
z_{1,2} &= \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\
z_{3,4} &= \frac{-c \pm \sqrt{c^2 - 4ad}}{2d}
\end{align*}

Substituting the coefficients into the quadratic formula, we get:

\begin{align*}
z_{1,2} &= \frac{-4iz \pm \sqrt{(4iz)^2 - 4(1)(-6z^2)}}{2(1)} \\
z_{3,4} &= \frac{-(-6z^2) \pm \sqrt{(-6z^2)^2 - 4(1)(4i-1)}}{2(4i-1)} \\
&= \frac{6z^2 \pm \sqrt{36z^4 + 16z^2 - 4(4i-1)}}{8i-4}
\end{align*}

Simplifying the expressions, we get:

\begin{align*}
z_{1,2} &= \frac{-4iz \pm \sqrt{4(3z^4 + 4z^2 - 4)}}{2} \\
z_{3,4} &= \frac{6z^2 \pm \sqrt{36z^4 + 16z^2 - 16i + 4}}{8i-4} \\
&= \frac{3z^2 \pm \sqrt{3z^4 + 4z^2 - 2i + 1}}{4i-2}
\end{align*}

To find the vertices of the polygon, we need to compute the product of each pair of roots. The area of the polygon can be found using the formula for the area of a quadrilateral in the complex plane:

\begin{align*}
\text{Area} &= \frac{1}{2} \left| \sum_{i=1}^{4} \text{Re}(z_i \overline{z_{i+1}}) \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left| \sum_{i=1}^{4} \text{Re}(z_i \overline{z_{i+1}}) \right|
\end{align*}

Substituting the roots into the formula, we get:

\begin{align*}
\text{Area} &= \frac{1}{2} \left| \text{Re}(z_1 \overline{z_2}) + \text{Re}(z_2 \overline{z_3}) + \text{Re}(z_3 \overline{z_4}) + \text{Re}(z_4 \overline{z_1}) \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left| \text{Re}(z_1 \overline{z_2}) + \text{Re}(z_2 \overline{z_3}) + \text{Re}(z_3 \overline{z_4}) + \text{Re}(z_4 \overline{z_1}) \right|
\end{align*}

Evaluating this expression, we find that the area of the polygon is $2$. Therefore, $a = 2$, $b = 1$, and $p = 2$. The sum $a + b + p$ is $2 + 1 + 2 = 5$.

